.TH SYN 4
.SH NAME
syn, ts \- syntax programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
[
.B -q
.I highlights.scm
]
.PP
.B /bin/ts/pic
.SH DESCRIPTION
A syntax program reads documents in one language and answers questions about them:
the runs that color its text, its parse, its definitions and references, and its tokens.
.IR Hl (1)
and
.IR Tree (1)
run one for each language they meet, once, and keep it for every document in that language.
Each
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
is a program on a tree-sitter grammar, and
.B /bin/ts/pic
a lexer for
.IR pic (1)'s
pictures, with no grammar.
.PP
Rules, in
.B $home/lib/syn
or, if there is none,
.BR /lib/syn ,
say which program serves a file.
They are
.IR ndb (6)
entries.
An entry
.BI syn= name
gives a language, by the name that
.I hl
and Markdown's fenced blocks use:
.TF fence=
.TP
.B cmd=
the command that runs its program, by
.IR rc (1)
.TP
.B file=
a regular expression
.RI ( regexp (6))
for the full names of the files in the language; the first entry that matches serves a file
.TP
.B fence=
the name of a fenced block in Markdown that the language colors; an entry may have several
.PD
.PP
For example,
.IP
.EX
syn=go	cmd=/bin/ts/go
	file="\e.go$"
	fence=go fence=golang
.EE
.SS Requests
A program reads requests on its standard input and answers some on its standard output.
A request names a document by a number,
.IR id ,
and counts places in it in runes.
.TF delete\ id\ q0\ q1
.TP
.BI body " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, the text of document
.IR id ;
answered by
.BI runs " id m"
and
.I m
bytes of runs, in the form of
.IR astyles (4)'s
.B style
file.
The program keeps each document's parse, so that a change is parsed again only where it is.
.TP
.BI insert " id q n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, put in document
.I id
at rune
.IR q .
.TP
.BI delete " id q0 q1"
takes the runes
.I q0
to
.I q1
from it.
.TP
.BI span " id q0 q1"
answered by
.BI span " id r0 r1 m"
and
.I m
bytes of the runs of the runes
.I r0
to
.IR r1 ,
counted from
.IR r0 :
the text whose runs may have changed since the last answer, and that of
.I q0
to
.IR q1 ;
or by
.BI stale " id"
if an edit failed.
.TP
.BI tree " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, the text of document
.IR id ;
answered by
.BI tree " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: a line for each named node of the parse, root first and each node before its children,
.IP
.IB "depth start length type field"
.IP
where
.I depth
counts the named nodes above it,
.I start
and
.I length
are in runes,
and
.I field
is the name the grammar gives the node's place in its parent, or
.LR - .
.TP
.BI tree " id"
with no text, answered by
.BI delta " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: what changed in the parse since the last answer to
.BR tree ,
as hunks (see below).
.TP
.BI check " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, a text parsed alone and not kept; answered by
.BI check " id k n\f1,"
where
.I k
counts the parse's error and missing nodes and
.I n
its named nodes.
.TP
.BI tags " id n"
followed by
.I n
bytes, the text of document
.IR id ,
or with no
.I n
the text as the program keeps it;
answered by
.BI tags " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: a line for each match of the language's
.BR tags.scm ,
.IP
.IB "start length type kind name-start name-length"
.IP
where
.I kind
is the match's capture, such as
.B definition.function
or
.BR reference.call ,
and the name is its
.B @name
capture's text;
or, if the language has no
.BR tags.scm ,
by
.BI error " id why"\f1.
.TP
.BI tokens " id q0 q1"
answered by
.BI tokens " id m"
and
.I m
bytes: a line for each token that overlaps the runes
.I q0
to
.IR q1 ,
in order,
.IP
.IB "start length names tags"
.IP
where
.I start
and
.I length
are in runes,
.I names
are the token's own names joined by commas,
and
.I tags
the names of the ranges it is inside, joined by commas, or
.L -
for none (see Tokens below).
.TP
.BI close " id"
forgets document
.IR id .
.PD
.PP
The program answers an error with
.BI error " id why"\f1.
The program keeps a document's parse from one request to the next, whichever they are.
For the C text
.LR "int x = f(1);" ,
.B tree
gives
.IP
.EX
0 0 14 translation_unit -
1 0 13 declaration -
2 0 3 primitive_type type
2 4 8 init_declarator declarator
3 4 1 identifier declarator
3 8 4 call_expression value
4 8 1 identifier function
4 9 3 argument_list arguments
5 10 1 number_literal -
.EE
.PP
which tree-sitter writes as
.IP
.EX
(translation_unit (declaration type: (primitive_type)
  declarator: (init_declarator declarator: (identifier)
    value: (call_expression function: (identifier)
      arguments: (argument_list (number_literal))))))
.EE
.PP
and
.B tokens
gives
.IP
.EX
0 3 type,ident -
4 1 variable,ident -
6 1 operator,oper -
8 1 function,ident reference.call
9 1 punct.bracket reference.call
10 1 number reference.call
11 1 punct.bracket reference.call
12 1 punctuation.delimiter,punct.delimiter -
.EE
.PP
A
.B delta
answer is the last
.B tree
or
.B delta
answer brought up to date.
Each line of that answer not in a hunk stays, with its start and end moved by the
.B insert
and
.B delete
requests since, in order.
An insert of
.I k
runes at
.I q
moves a start at or after
.I q
and an end after
.I q
by
.IR k ,
so it moves a node that starts there and does not lengthen one that ends there.
A delete of
.I q0
to
.I q1
moves a place at or after
.I q1
back by
.IR q1 \- q0 ,
and one between them to
.IR q0 .
A hunk is a line
.IB "@ i k j"
followed by
.I j
lines in
.BR tree 's
form, which take the place of the
.I k
lines from line
.I i
(from 0) of the last answer; hunks come in the order of
.IR i .
If the program does not have the text, as after an edit past its end, it answers
.BR tree ,
.B tags
and
.B tokens
without text with
.BI error " id " lost\f1,
and wants the text again, as by
.BI tree " id n"\f1.
.PP
The document's program parses a text of another language inside it,
such as a fenced block of code in Markdown, if it has the language's grammar,
and that text's runs and tokens take the place of the document's there.
If it has not, the text's runs are one,
.BI ts/ lang\f1,
or
.BI ts/ lang / name
for the run that the text takes when there is no program for
.IR lang ;
.I hl
gives that text to the program of the rule with that fence.
.SS Tokens
A token is a leaf of the parse, named or not, such as a keyword, a bracket or a comment,
with the white space at its ends left out.
A lexer splits the text between leaves, as Markdown's inline text has,
and a named leaf that holds white space and is not a comment or a string,
into words, numbers and operators.
A token never crosses the edge of an injection's text.
A token's own names are its highlight name, if a highlight covers it, and its lexical name.
.PP
The highlight name is the name of a capture of the language's
.BR highlights.scm ,
as it colors the token, normalized by this table:
.IP
.EX
.ta \w'string.special.key  'u
field	property
parameter	variable.parameter
method	function.method
float	number.float
boolean	constant.builtin.boolean
include	keyword.import
conditional	keyword.conditional
repeat	keyword.repeat
exception	keyword.exception
storageclass	keyword.modifier
type.qualifier	keyword.modifier
namespace	module
escape	string.escape
delimiter	punctuation.delimiter
function.special	function.macro
string.special.key	property
text.title	markup.heading
text.literal	markup.raw
text.emphasis	markup.italic
text.strong	markup.bold
text.uri	markup.link.url
text.reference	markup.link.label
.EE
.PP
A name in the table, or one that begins with it and a dot, takes the name it gives,
with the rest after it:
.B method.call
becomes
.BR function.method.call .
A capture whose name begins with
.L _
or is
.BR none ,
.BR spell ,
.B nospell
or
.B conceal
names nothing.
Where captures overlap, the one that colors the text wins, as for runs, then the first;
but a later name that extends an earlier one, as
.B constant.macro
extends
.BR constant ,
takes its place.
The highlight names our queries give are
.BR attribute ,
.BR comment ,
.BR comment.documentation ,
.BR constant ,
.BR constant.builtin ,
.BR constant.builtin.boolean ,
.BR constant.macro ,
.BR constructor ,
.BR embedded ,
.BR function ,
.BR function.builtin ,
.BR function.call ,
.BR function.macro ,
.BR function.method ,
.BR keyword ,
.BR keyword.import ,
.BR label ,
.BR markup.bold ,
.BR markup.heading
and
.BR markup.heading.1
to
.BR .6 ,
.BR markup.html ,
.BR markup.italic ,
.BR markup.link.label ,
.BR markup.link.url ,
.BR markup.list ,
.BR markup.list.checked ,
.BR markup.list.unchecked ,
.BR markup.quote ,
.BR markup.raw.block ,
.BR markup.raw.delimiter ,
.BR markup.raw.inline ,
.BR markup.strikethrough ,
.BR markup.table ,
.BR number ,
.BR number.float ,
.BR operator ,
.BR property ,
.BR punctuation.bracket ,
.BR punctuation.delimiter ,
.BR punctuation.special ,
.BR string ,
.BR string.escape ,
.BR string.special ,
.BR type ,
.BR type.builtin ,
.BR variable ,
.BR variable.builtin
and
.BR variable.parameter .
.PP
Every token has a lexical name, after its highlight name unless the two are the same:
.TF punct.delimiter
.TP
.B comment
a comment
.TP
.B string
a string or character literal, or its text
.TP
.B escape
an escape in a string
.TP
.B number
a number
.TP
.B keyword
a token with letters that the grammar spells out, such as
.B return
or
.B #include
.TP
.B ident
any other word: letters, digits and
.LR _ ,
not starting with a digit, or any other runes but white space and ASCII's punctuation
.TP
.B punct.bracket
brackets, such as
.L (
or
.L [[
.TP
.B punct.delimiter
one
.LR , ,
.LR ; ,
.L .\&
or
.L :
.TP
.B punct.quote
quotes alone, such as a string's
.L \&"
.TP
.B oper
anything else, as
.L +=
or
.L ->
.PD
.PP
The tags are the kinds of the matches of the language's
.B tags.scm
whose ranges hold the token, outermost first, each once.
A match counts if it has a
.B @name
capture and a capture whose name begins
.B definition.
or
.BR reference. ;
its range is that capture's node.
The kinds our queries give are
.BR definition.class ,
.BR definition.constant ,
.BR definition.enum ,
.BR definition.function ,
.BR definition.interface ,
.BR definition.macro ,
.BR definition.method ,
.BR definition.module ,
.BR definition.node ,
.BR definition.object ,
.BR definition.property ,
.BR definition.type ,
.BR definition.variable ,
.BR reference.call ,
.BR reference.class ,
.BR reference.edge ,
.BR reference.implementation ,
.BR reference.interface
and
.BR reference.type .
A Mermaid diagram's nodes are
.BR definition.node ,
and its edges, messages and transitions
.BR reference.edge .
A program that needs another name adds it here.
.SS Queries
A grammar's program colors the captures of its query,
.IB lang /highlights.scm\f1,
by tree-sitter's query language with its
.BR #eq? ,
.B #match?
and
.B #any-of?
predicates and their
.B not-
forms.
Where captures overlap, the first one wins.
A capture's run takes the name that
.B names
gives its capture, or the capture with its last
.BI . part
taken off, and so on; a capture with no name has no run.
.IB lang /injections.scm
gives the texts of other languages inside a document, and
.IB lang /tags.scm
its definitions and references.
The program reads each of these files from
.B $home/lib/ts
in place of
.BR /sys/lib/ts .
The
.B -q
option reads the highlights query from a file instead.
.PP
Any program that speaks this protocol can stand in for one.
.B /bin/ts/pic
is one, for pictures' words, labels, numbers, strings and comments.
It has no parse: it answers
.BR tree ,
.B tags
and
.B check
with an error, and
.B tokens
from its lexer, with the names
.BR keyword ,
.BR label ,
.BR number ,
.BR string ,
.BR comment ,
and the lexical names of Tokens, and no tags.
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/lib/ts/lang/highlights.scm
.TP
.B /lib/syn
the languages' rules
.TP
.B /sys/lib/ts/names
capture names to
.IR theme (6)
names
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /highlights.scm
a language's highlights
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /tags.scm
its definitions and references
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /injections.scm
the other languages inside it
.TP
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
a language's program
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/ts/ts.c
and
.BR src/cmd/ts/pic.c ;
tree-sitter's runtime and grammars from
.BR scripts/dl.sh ,
and the queries and names from acme-treesitter, with our own additions
.SH SEE ALSO
